CART AXIOM SYSTEM
=================
A coordinate‑free algebra relational tensioner

CART = (C, K, D, C/K, H)

This document defines the axioms governing the five components:
Carrier, Kernel, Defect, Quotient, and Hierarchy.


------------------------------------------------------------
1. CARRIER AXIOMS — RAW RELATIONAL FIELD
------------------------------------------------------------

Axiom C1 — Existence
C is a nonempty set of relational configurations.

Axiom C2 — Coordinate‑Free
Elements of C have no coordinates, geometry, metric, or embedding.

Axiom C3 — Closure
C is closed under its native relational operations (if any are defined).

Axiom C4 — Pre‑Resolution
No element of C is assumed consistent, canonical, or admissible prior to K.


------------------------------------------------------------
2. KERNEL AXIOMS — TENSION‑RESOLUTION OPERATOR
------------------------------------------------------------

Axiom K1 — Endomorphism
K : C → C is a total function.

Axiom K2 — Idempotence
For all x ∈ C:
    K(K(x)) = K(x)
(Once resolved, stays resolved.)

Axiom K3 — Contractivity
For all x ∈ C:
    K(x) is relationally no more tense than x.
(Formally: D(K(x)) ≤ D(x) once D is defined.)

Axiom K4 — Canonicality
K(x) is the canonical representative of the identity class of x.

Axiom K5 — Stability
If x is already canonical, then K(x) = x.


------------------------------------------------------------
3. DEFECT AXIOMS — RESIDUAL RELATIONAL TENSION
------------------------------------------------------------

Axiom D1 — Defect Map
D : C → Δ, where Δ is a defect domain.

Axiom D2 — Kernel Agreement
For all x ∈ C:
    D(x) = 0  ⇔  x = K(x)
(Zero defect means fully resolved.)

Axiom D3 — Residual Tension
For all x ∈ C:
    D(x) ≥ 0
(Defect is nonnegative in its domain.)

Axiom D4 — Deviation
D(x) depends only on the relational mismatch between x and K(x).

Axiom D5 — Monotonicity Under Resolution
For all x ∈ C:
    D(K(x)) ≤ D(x)


------------------------------------------------------------
4. QUOTIENT AXIOMS — IDENTITY FORMATION
------------------------------------------------------------

Axiom Q1 — Kernel Equivalence
Define x ~ y  iff  K(x) = K(y).

Axiom Q2 — Equivalence Relation
The relation ~ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.

Axiom Q3 — Identity Classes
C/K is the set of equivalence classes [x] under ~.

Axiom Q4 — Canonical Representative
For each class [x], K(x) is the unique canonical representative.

Axiom Q5 — Admissibility
A state x is admissible iff:
    D(x) ≤ ε
for some fixed threshold ε.

Axiom Q6 — Class Admissibility
A class [x] is admissible iff at least one (equivalently all) of its members
satisfy D(x) ≤ ε.


------------------------------------------------------------
5. HIERARCHY AXIOMS — RELATIONAL ENTANGLEMENT DEPTH
------------------------------------------------------------

Axiom H1 — Ultrametric Structure
H assigns an ultrametric u to admissible classes in C/K.

Axiom H2 — Ultrametric Inequality
For all classes A, B, C:
    u(A, C) ≤ max(u(A, B), u(B, C))

Axiom H3 — Tiered Depth
H partitions classes into ordinal tiers:
    Tier‑1 (shallow)
    Tier‑2 (intermediate)
    Tier‑3 (deep)

Axiom H4 — Depth Monotonicity
If u(A, B) < u(A, C), then depth(A) < depth(C).

Axiom H5 — Identity Preservation
If K(x) = K(y), then u([x], [y]) = 0.

Axiom H6 — Entanglement Interpretation
Depth corresponds to relational entanglement complexity, not geometry.


------------------------------------------------------------
6. INTEGRATED CART AXIOM
------------------------------------------------------------
For all x ∈ C:

    (1) K resolves relational tension.
    (2) D measures residual tension.
    (3) C/K forms identity classes via kernel equality.
    (4) H organizes identity classes by entanglement depth.
    (5) All operations are coordinate‑free and purely relational.

This integrated axiom ensures the five components operate as a single,
coherent, coordinate‑free relational tension algebra.


------------------------------------------------------------
7. ONE-LINE CART AXIOMATIC SUMMARY
------------------------------------------------------------
CART is a coordinate‑free algebra in which:
- the Carrier holds raw relational configurations,
- the Kernel resolves relational tension,
- the Defect measures residual tension,
- the Quotient forms identity classes,
- and the Hierarchy orders those identities by relational entanglement depth.
